A||||6||Ed||Black respray||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||04-20-2001||12:22 AM||||For you painters out there:<P>Is it practical to change the color of my black Fiero?  Or am I stuck with black if I want to repaint the car?  <P>If I were to have it painted, it would be a good "production" job, probably not customized with ten or twenty coats of candy apple red.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Neverfinished||04-20-2001||02:12 AM||||I repainted my white SE a pearl mettalic purple and only the body panels were white.<BR>Most of the areas like under the hood and decklid are were black already so any color would work in my opinion. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||04-20-2001||09:31 AM||||The good thing about changing whole body colors with Fieros is that you can take all the body panels off, paint them, and nobody would be the wiser as to what the color was before.  Normal cars have the entire unibody, engine compartment, etc painted at the factory, so it is very very hard to change colors the right way.  For instance, if you took, say a black grand prix, and wanted it red, you'd still probably have a black engine compartment and black door jambs.<P>There are still some problems to run into with painting Fieros if you dont want to take off the body panels.  There are still some little pieces that are covered by a shut door where paint wont be able to fog into with your new color (rear 1/4 panels, about a 1" lip covered by a shut door, 1" lip on fenders covered by hood and a decklid)<P>So basically, it can be done, but to do a really nice job, you'd have to take apart the body panels.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Fiero87311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto near completion! :D<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||3FastbackGTs||04-20-2001||05:02 PM||||  color changing a fiero is no harder to do  than doing the dong the same color properly. I just painted my 86 GT GM Frost white over the original OEM white &lt;two different shades of white&gt; the process is the same. last fall I color changed my 87 GT from gold to silver<BR>with no trouble at all and you would be hard pressed to find any signs of it ever being any other color than silver. when I paint Fieros..I remover the hood, decklid, wing, mirrors, headlight covers and rear bumper cover. I hang the deck lid, hood, and spoiler vertically to be painted &lt;dirt doesn't fall sideways&gt; it makes it much easier to shoot that way. As far as removing any other panels..there really is no need..remove all the bolts running down each side of the fenders and simply slide your masking paper under the fender lip. The door jambs are no problem either...just takes a little time to mask. shot the jambs first..cose te doors and proceed shooting the rest of the car. I will post pics ofmy cars and the painting process when I get time..good luck!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||04-20-2001||06:06 PM||||Hey Ed!<BR>Just had my black 86 repainted california gold...<BR>The painter cut in the doors and such, plus he did the mirrors...<BR>Looks killer!!!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||04-20-2001||06:44 PM||||Under the yellow paint, my '85 SE has panels that were silver, red, white, and black.  I think primer covers any color.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero_Freak||04-22-2001||11:13 AM||||I've always used a good sealer, then primer and then paint and clearcoat.  The sealer just helps things from bleeding through.  Although this will be the first time I've painted a Fiero, I still think I'll pull everything off.  And hanging it up is definately the way to go.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
